      "test_set": "The four models on two generated sim devices (seeds 7 and 13, depth route), one run each, with a random planted fault (fault seeds 7001 and 13001) and a 5% chance per action that the arm or the world misbehaves (error seed 1). The rule-based baseline ran on the same set. Every model got the same devices and faults. Errors were then drawn from a running count of actions, so models that planned differently met different errors: every model run drew one error, and both baseline runs drew a device move. Coverage tops out at 94% because device 7's knob needs a pinch grip, which the robot's turn skill doesn't take: every model reported it as untestable.",
      "spend": "570,906 tokens across the four models (no Cortex prices set, so no dollar figure).",
      "takeaway": "All four models found both planted faults, against none for the rule baseline, which stopped at a device move it can't plan around. Grok re-explored after a real device move and finished the plan. The differences were in judgment: Haiku made up a 20 mm travel limit and reported a working slider as wrong feedback; GPT-5.4 made up a contact-offset bound and got the slider called intermittent; Haiku pushed a jammed door again at 12 N and hit the 15 N safety cap, then homed correctly. Grok took about 10 s a turn, against 2 to 5 s for the others.",
